  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Three-point, the starting grammar', note: 'Key (the main source, motivated by a window or lamp), fill (softens shadows — its absence is a style), back (separates subject from background). Classic portraiture to interviews: three lights, infinite moods by ratio.' },
    { math: '2 · Hard vs soft', note: 'Small sources cast hard shadows (noir, interrogation, truth-telling), large sources soft (romance, memory, beauty). Diffusion (silks, bounce) and the inverse-square law — the physics lesson\'s light falloff — make a cinematographer\'s toolkit physics.' },
    { math: '3 · Ratio = tone', note: 'Low-key (high contrast, deep blacks — thrillers, noir) vs high-key (bright, flat, few shadows — comedy, commercials): the contrast ratio IS the genre signal, read before a line is spoken (the visual-arts lesson\'s value structure, in motion).' },
    { math: '4 · Colour and motivation', note: 'Teal-and-orange blockbusters (skin against cool), the fluorescent institutional green of a hospital drama, the sodium-street amber of insomnia — the colour lesson\'s schemes as narrative. Motivated lighting: every light should have a plausible source in the world (or a reason to break the rule).' },
  ],
};
