<TopicPage slug="subjects/film/intermediate/cinematography-and-lighting">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cinematography is <strong>lighting plus composition as continuous authorship</strong> — the
    visual-arts ladder\'s value, colour and framing, applied 24 times a second. The best
    cinematographers are painters whose paintings move and obey budgets.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="three-point, hard/soft, ratio, colour" widget="equationStep" config={cineCfg} />

  <h2>The masters\' lessons</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Gregg Toland\'s deep focus (Citizen Kane):</strong> everything sharp — foreground and background arguing; the visual lesson\'s history chapter had Orson Welles arguing with space itself (deep-focus staging replaced by cutting elsewhere).</li>
    <li><strong> available light as aesthetic:</strong> naturalistic movements (the Dardenne brothers, A24\'s newer realism) treat practical lights as the whole scheme — the constraint lesson\'s creative narrowness, photographic edition.</li>
    <li><strong>Aspect, format and texture:</strong> 70 mm grandeur, 16 mm grain as memory, digital\'s clean darkness — format choice is tonal (the shot lesson\'s ratio logic, plus grain as time-period marker).</li>
    <li><strong>Virtual production arrived:</strong> LED walls replacing green screen (The Mandalorian) — the lighting matches the background because the background IS the light source; the film lesson\'s grammar, absorbing game-engine tech (the AI ladder\'s graphics wing).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> American Cinematographer interviews; Blain Brown, Cinematography: Theory and Practice; the visual-arts ladder.</p>
</TopicPage>
